# `dist` above matches at any depth, which would also swallow the vendored
# @ravn/ui-kit build CI depends on — see vendor/ravn-ui-kit/README.md for why
# it's committed rather than built from a sibling checkout.
!vendor/ravn-ui-kit/dist/
!vendor/ravn-ui-kit/dist/**
coverage
*.local
.DS_Store
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea
.claude/settings.local.json
UI_KIT_MIGRATION_PLAN.md
UI_KIT_MIGRATION_NEXT_PROMPT.md

# corvus — managed, do not edit this block
#
# One deliberate edit, which `corvus install` may undo: the `.corvusrc` line
# this block ships with has been removed. The file is tracked, and a tracked
# file ignores .gitignore entirely, so the entry was inert *and* contradicted
# the tracking — read either way round, one of the two was wrong.
#
# It is resolved in favour of tracking. `.corvusrc` is the skill manifest, and
# only because it is tracked does it reach every `git worktree`, which is the
# only reason `corvus sync` can restore the 15 skills in a fresh worker
# checkout. Untracking it would leave each new session with no skills and no
# way to discover which ones this project expects.
#
# If a future `corvus install` rewrites this block and puts the line back, the
# tracking still wins — delete the line again rather than untracking the file.
.claude/skills/core-coding-standards/
.claude/skills/lang-typescript/
.claude/skills/platform-frontend/
.claude/skills/tech-react/
.claude/skills/design-frontend/
.claude/skills/design-accessibility/
.claude/skills/platform-testing/
.claude/skills/tech-vitest/
.claude/skills/figma-to-react-components/
.claude/skills/agent-pr-creator/
.claude/skills/grill-me/
.claude/skills/promptify/
.claude/skills/qa-bug-fixer/
.claude/skills/qa-happy-path/
.claude/skills/design-variations/
# end corvus

# The local project link, and the OIDC token `vercel link` writes into
# `.env.local`. The `.env` rules near the top already cover the latter — the
# CLI also appends its own `.env*`, which has to be removed each time, because
# landing after `!.env.example` it quietly re-ignores the one env file this
# repo does track.
.vercel
